 study design. These domains were used to objectively measure the comp
lexity of a study protocol.
Scoring and Decision Approach: Individual domain scores and a total co
mplexity score (TCS) were calculated. Post-PCT pass through, the TCS w
as reduced in 75% of trials. Time-to-site activation and participant e
nrolment positively correlated with TCS, reaching statistical signific
ance at 75% site activation and 25% participant recruitment. The metad
ata does not specify go/no-go decisions, specific numeric scales, weig
hted scoring, categorical levels, or explicit decision thresholds/cuto
ffs. Not specified.
Results: *   The Total Complexity Score (TCS) was reduced in 12 trials
 (75%), remained the same in 3 trials (18.8%), and increased in 1 tria
l (6.2%) post-PCT pass through.
*   Complexity decreased most notably in the operational execution (50
% of trials) and site burden (43.8% of trials) domains.
*   Time-to-site activation and participant enrolment positively corre
lated with TCS, reaching statistical significance at 75% site activati
on (rho = 0.61; p = 0.005; n = 19) and 25% participant recruitment (rh
o = 0.59; p = 0.012; n = 17).
Findings: *   A Protocol Complexity Tool (PCT) was developed to object
ively measure protocol complexity.
*   The PCT reduced the total complexity score in 12 out of 16 trials 
(75%) post-pass through.
*   Complexity decreased most notably in operational execution (50% of
 trials) and site burden (43.8% of trials) domains.
*   Total complexity score positively correlated with time-to-site act
ivation (rho = 0.61; p = 0.005; n = 19) and participant enrolment (rho
 = 0.59; p = 0.012; n = 17).
