 start-up delays in global, randomized clinical trials, which can be s
een as complexity dimensions. These categories include:

*   Regulatory
*   Contracts and budgets
*   Insurance
*   Clinical supplies
*   Site identification and selection
*   Site activation
*   Inefficient processes/pitfalls
Scoring and Decision Approach: Not addressed in this paper.
Results: *   The literature review included 89 peer-reviewed journal a
rticles and supplemental industry white papers and a book.
*   Key drivers for start-up delays in randomized controlled trials (R
CTs) relate to regulatory approvals, site contracts and budgets, insur
ance, clinical supplies, site activation, inefficient processes, CROs,
 and translations.
*   The key findings in each of these areas are discussed in the paper
.
Findings: *   The literature review identified 89 peer-reviewed journa
l articles, industry white papers, and a book.
*   Key drivers for start-up delays in randomized controlled trials (R
CTs) include regulatory approvals, site contracts and budgets, insuran
ce, clinical supplies, site activation, inefficient processes, CROs, a
nd translations.
*   These key drivers were detailed in a fishbone diagram (Fig. 1).
