AB  - When study sponsors assess the feasibility of a study, there are two p
arts to the exercise: (a) protocol-level feasibility (analysis of the 
study design) and (b) site-level feasibility, which is closely linked 
to the site selection process. Unfortunately, many feasibility assessm
ents are not conducted in a thorough, consistent and systematic manner
 that accounts for real-world factors and supports process improvement
 from study to study. This article will discuss: x Common reasons feas
ibility assessments fail to result in predictable and timely study per
formance x Differences between protocol- and site-level feasibility
