AB  - I n this issue of Pediatric Blood & Cancer (page nnn), Nimmer and coll
eagues present a method of preconsent to enhance accrual to a randomiz
ed, double-blind, placebo-controlled clinical trial, which assigned ch
ildren with sickle cell vasoocclusive crises to receive intravenous ma
gnesium or normal saline placebo (magnesium in crisis [MAGiC] trial). 
While this study did not demonstrate benefit from the use of intraveno
us magnesium in terms of length of hospital stay, opioid use, or quali
ty of life, the authors did gain valuable insight into research method
ology in this population in the emergency setting.[1] Children with si
ckle cell disease have historically been poorly served in North Americ
an emergency departments due to knowledge deficits about the disease i
tself, in addition to the challenges for racial minorities in accessin
g timely and quality care. Collecting valuable data with the aim of im
proving care and outcomes for such populations is also complicated for
 various reasons. African-American and pediatric patients have been sh
own to be less likely to enroll on available clinical trials in a larg
e academic center in North Carolina.[2] Mistrust is well known to impa
ct recruitment of racial minorities to clinical trials.[3,4] Informed 
consent for research for patients in emergency departments might not b
e obtained due to the patient’s clinical status or the lack of an avai
lable legal authorized representative. Time constraints and the stress
 associated with acute medical complications (such as pain) are additi
onal challenges. Logistics such as time of day and research staff avai
lability may limit accrual, particularly when collaboration with addit
ional services (e.g., pediatric hematology) is required. Such barriers
 do not absolve clinicians and care providers of their duty to include
 disadvantaged populations in clinical research—these barriers must be
 identified to allow for the development of strategies to overcome the
m. In fact, both National Institute of Health in the United States and
 Canadian Tri-Council Policy Statement on the Ethical Conduct for Rese
arch Involving Humans state that minorities and vulnerable populations
 should not be inappropriately excluded from research that might provi
de them benefit, although additional safeguardsmight be required for t
heir protection.[5,6] One strategy to improve the education and unders
tanding of potential research participants is preconsent. This process
 involves time spent with the research team and/or multimedia educatio
nal materials—in advance of eligibility for enrollment on the trial or
 randomization—which might include a description of general principles
 of clinical research or specific components of a particular clinical 
trial. Preconsent takes place in a calm, controlled environment with n
o time pressures, consistent with the process of truly informed consen
t. A study of this process in two cohorts of adult African Americans o
ffered a hypothetical research trial with and without preconsent educa
tional material. In those preconsented, significantly fewer subjects c
ited mistrust, fear of adverse effects, perceived lack of benefits, an
d privacy concerns as reasons for declining the hypothetical study.[7]
 These findings are consistent with a report of lessons learned from a
 trial in children and adults performed by the Sickle Cell Disease Cli
nical Research Network, in which sites that enrolled subjects strongly
 endorsed the statement that “educating subjects aided enrollment.”[4]
 Informed consent practices for research involving children with leuke
mia have improved with the guidance of parents of such children, with 
conclusions from qualitative research emphasizing the importance of ti
me to make decisions, the provision of additional materials, organized
 consent conferences, and the staging of consent over several meetings
.[8] The authors suggest that their findings should not differ fundame
ntally from the needs of children and families with other diagnoses, p
articularly when time pressure and stressmight influence the informed 
consent process. With these barriers in mind, combined with the litera
ture describing preconsent as one potential solution to enhance the un
derstanding of prospective subjects, Nimmer et al. employed a preconse
nt process at some sites in the MAGiC trial. Four of eight sites imple
mented such a process, with the remaining sites citing resource challe
nges and prior experiences or knowledge of the limitations of preconse
nt as reasons to decline its implementation. The authors found that pr
econsented subjects were much more likely to undergo randomization com
pared to those whose informed consent process took place in its entire
ty upon presentation with a vasoocclusive crisis. Few patients who pre
consented to participate in the study later declined ongoing participa
tion when presenting in crisis. These data suggest a strong willingnes
s of those subjects who agreed to participate in the preconsent proces
s to undergo randomization, but it would be
