AB  - Numerous studies demonstrate associations between serum concentrations
 of 25-hydroxyvitamin D (25[OH]D) and a variety of common disorders, i
ncluding musculoskeletal, metabolic, cardiovascular, malignant, autoim
mune, and infectious diseases. Although a causal link between serum 25
(OH)D concentrations and many disorders has not been clearly establish
ed, these associations have led to widespread supplementation with vit
amin D and increased laboratory testing for 25(OH)D in the general pop
ulation. The benefit-risk ratio of this increase in vitamin D use is n
ot clear, and the optimal vitamin D intake and the role of testing for
 25(OH)D for disease prevention remain uncertain.
